---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Container from "../components/Container.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import CTA from "../components/CTA.astro";
import { site } from "../lib/site.js";
---
<Base
  title="About"
  description="Why Thryft Shop exists: listing is the bottleneck between owning something and selling it, and it should not take twenty minutes an item."
>
  <div class="brand-wash border-b border-line">
    <Container>
      <div class="max-w-3xl py-16 sm:py-20">
        <p class="font-display text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-blue-ink">
          About
        </p>
        <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl sm:text-5xl">Listing is the bottleneck</h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-ink-secondary">
          Not sourcing, not shipping, not pricing. The reason there is a pile of
          sellable things in the corner of your room is that turning each one
          into a listing takes twenty minutes of typing you don't want to do.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </div>

  <Container>
    <div class="prose max-w-3xl py-14 sm:py-16">
      <p>
        Thryft Shop exists to collapse that twenty minutes into a photo. Take the
        picture; the app identifies the item, writes the title and description,
        fills in the item specifics marketplace search actually filters on,
        resolves a real category, suggests a price, cleans up the images to the
        marketplace's own recommendations — and then hands the whole thing back
        to you to check before anything goes live.
      </p>

      <h2>Who it's for</h2>
      <p>
        Resellers running a real store, thrifters who source faster than they can
        list, and anyone clearing out a closet who would rather not type out
        listings by hand. The single-item case is easy. What makes the difference
        is volume: a pile of photos becoming a pile of drafts, a store that syncs
        both ways, duplicates found and merged, and a ranked list of what to fix
        next across everything you have live.
      </p>

      <h2>What we won't do</h2>
      <p>
        Publish without you. Every draft is reviewable and every field is
        editable, and the app tells you what it was unsure about rather than
        presenting a guess as a fact. You are the seller — what goes live is
        your name on it, so it is your decision.
      </p>
      <p>
        We also don't sell your data, use your photos for advertising, or hold
        your account hostage. Deletion is a button in Settings that works
        immediately, not a support ticket.
      </p>

      <h2>How it's built</h2>
      <p>
        Claude's vision models do the identification and the writing. The photo
        pipeline is our own, tuned against the failure that actually costs
        sellers money — a background cutout that quietly removes part of the
        product. Publishing goes through each marketplace's real API, with every
        marketplace succeeding or failing independently so one rejection never
        takes the rest down with it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Container>

  <Section tone="sunken" align="left" title="Get in touch">
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-ink-secondary">
      Feature requests, bug reports, and "why did it do that" questions are all
      welcome at
      <a class="font-semibold text-blue-ink hover:underline" href={`mailto:${site.supportEmail}`}>{site.supportEmail}</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <CTA />
</Base>
